<article class="env-theorem" id="042G">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/042G">Theorem <span data-tag="042G">8.5.9.4</span>.</a> Let $n \geq 3$ be an integer. The inclusion map $\operatorname{N}_{\leq n}( \operatorname{Idem}) \hookrightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Idem})$ admits a factorization </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{N}_{\leq n}(\operatorname{Idem}) \xrightarrow { \iota } \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\xrightarrow { U } \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Idem}), \]
</div>
<p> where $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ is an $\infty $-category, $\iota $ is an inner anodyne morphism which is bijective on simplices of dimension $&lt; n$, and the functor $U$ admits a right inverse $V: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Idem}) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof of Theorem <a data-tag="042G" href="/tag/042G">8.5.9.4</a>.</strong>
       Fix an integer $n \geq 0$. Using Example <a data-tag="042R" href="/tag/042R">8.5.9.12</a>, we can choose an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ and an inner anodyne morphism $\iota : \operatorname{N}_{\leq n}(\operatorname{Idem}) \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ which is bijective on simplices of dimension $&lt; n$. Since $\iota $ is inner anodyne, there is a unique functor $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Idem})$ for which $U \circ \iota $ coincides with the inclusion map $\operatorname{N}_{\leq n}( \operatorname{Idem}) \hookrightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Idem})$. If $n \geq 3$, then Proposition <a data-tag="042S" href="/tag/042S">8.5.9.13</a> guarantees that $\iota (\widetilde{e})$ is an idempotent endomorphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$: that is, there exists a functor $V: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Idem}) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ satisfying $V(\widetilde{e}) = \iota (\widetilde{e})$. To complete the proof, it will suffice to show that the composition $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Idem}) \xrightarrow {V} \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\xrightarrow {U} \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Idem})$ is the identity functor. This follows from the universal property of Remark <a data-tag="03ZP" href="/tag/03ZP">8.5.2.8</a> (together with Proposition <a data-tag="002Z" href="/tag/002Z">1.3.3.1</a>), since the functor $U \circ V$ carries the morphism $e$ to itself. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
